# Review: Expert-Guided Cross-View Fusion With Self-Derived Lesion Proposals For Multi-View Diabetic Retinopathy Grading

---

## Critical Issues

### Issue 1 — Backbone Confound Undermines the Primary Comparative Claim (Structural)

**Sections: §4.1 Implementation Details, Tables 1–3.**

The central headline claim is that the proposed framework "matches or surpasses" externally-informed methods without using external annotations. However, all prior baselines (MVCINN, MVCNN R/V, ETMC, CVSA, WGLIN, LFMVDR, CrossFiT, etc.) use architectures ranging from ResNet50 to custom CNN-Transformer hybrids, while this paper uses Swin-B — a substantially stronger backbone. The ablation confirms that even the most stripped-down variant of the proposed method still outperforms nearly all prior end-to-end methods: "w/o LGRF" (no cross-view fusion module, just GALP with simple concatenation) achieves 82.3%, and "w/o GALP" (LGRF on all tokens) achieves 82.7%, compared to ETMC's 81.5% and MVCINN's 80.1%. The single-view Swin-B baseline (Table 5, Appendix) already reaches 75.0%, which exceeds single-view equivalents of ResNet and VGG used in MVCNN baselines.

The paper provides no backbone-controlled comparison: it never reports "Swin-B + naive multi-view fusion (concatenation only)" as a standalone baseline. Without this, it is impossible to determine how much of the 83.9% result comes from the two proposed modules (GALP + LGRF) versus the stronger backbone that no competitor uses. The gains attributed to the proposed method may largely reflect an architecture upgrade rather than the contributions claimed.

This is a structural flaw because the primary comparative narrative — annotation-free matching of annotation-using methods — rests on comparisons where backbone parity is not established.

---

### Issue 2 — Lesion Proposal Quality Never Validated Against Available Ground Truth (Evidential)

**Sections: §3.2 GALP, §4.1 Datasets, §4.2.**

The paper's core conceptual contribution is that CAM-derived grade-conditioned evidence maps (GEMs) serve as "surrogates for external lesion cues." This claim is central to the framing of the entire paper. However, the MFIDDR dataset explicitly provides lesion segmentation masks (§4.1: "The provider also releases lesion segmentation masks generated by a segmentation model"). Despite having ground-truth lesion annotations available, the paper never measures whether the Top-K proposal regions selected by GALP actually overlap with known lesion locations (e.g., no IoU, recall, or precision against the provided masks is reported).

The absence of this validation is not minor. Class activation maps produced from grade classifiers are grade-discriminative, not necessarily lesion-localized. They can activate on any spatially correlated feature — optic disc boundaries, vessel density patterns, overall image brightness variations — that happens to predict grade. Without localization validation, the framing of GEMs as "lesion proposals" is an assertion, not a demonstrated property. The entire downstream justification for restricting cross-view fusion to proposal tokens (rather than all tokens) hinges on whether those tokens actually encode lesion evidence; if they do not, the proposed architecture is simply a structured form of Top-K feature selection whose functional benefit is unrelated to lesion localization.

This is a large evidential gap. The validation data exists in the benchmark and was not used.

---

### Issue 3 — Ablation Does Not Isolate Module Contributions From Architecture Baseline (Structural)

**Section: §4.3, Table 4.**

The ablation table compares four variants: full model (83.9%), w/o GALP (82.7%), w/o Experts (82.6%), w/o LGRF (82.3%). All four variants surpass all end-to-end baselines except their own full model. This pattern indicates that the backbone architecture plus some form of cross-view attention already exceeds prior work — and neither GALP nor LGRF individually accounts for this gap.

Critically, Table 4 does not include the true baseline: Swin-B encoder + global average pooling + multi-view concatenation, with no GALP, no LGRF, and no cross-view attention. Without this anchor point, it is not possible to determine how much of the performance above prior baselines originates from (a) the stronger backbone, (b) the cross-view attention mechanism itself (which is present even in "w/o GALP"), or (c) the specific lesion-proposal routing mechanism (which is the claimed contribution). The ablations as designed measure the marginal value of each module on top of an already-sophisticated system, not the absolute contribution of the proposed ideas.

---

## Section-by-Section Notes

**§1 Introduction / §2 Related Work — Stage III framing overstated.** The paper introduces a "Stage III" of the field defined as "revisiting end-to-end limitations." This framing implies a natural scientific progression that the proposed work fulfills. In practice, the paper's approach is a specific architectural choice (CAM + MoE routing), not a paradigm shift. The rhetorical structure sets up a contrast (Stage II end-to-end methods vs. Stage III annotation-augmented methods) and then positions the proposed work as a third option, but this topology is author-constructed and does not accurately reflect the prior literature, where hybrid and semi-supervised approaches have co-existed throughout.

**§3.1 Overview — Cyclic cross-view fusion is unjustified.** Section 3.3 states: "Cross-view fusion is performed between the current view and its adjacent (cyclic) view j = i+1, i<N; 1, i=N." For 4-view MFIDDR, this means each view only fuses with one other view (1→2, 2→3, 3→4, 4→1). There is no clinical or geometric justification for why only adjacent views provide complementary information — DR lesions visible in view 1 may be most corroborated by view 3, not view 2. This choice is not ablated. An all-pairs or learned attention over views would be the natural comparison.

**§3.2 GALP — "Grade-conditioned" vs. "lesion-conditioned" conflation.** The paper states: "Since the grade evidence in DR is predominantly localized to lesions, selecting Top-K peaks...yields lesion proposals." This is a plausible hypothesis, not a demonstration. The CAM weights w are class-specific to the *predicted* grade (not ground truth), so if early-layer classifiers produce incorrect predictions (especially for minority grades like Grade 1 and Grade 3, where all methods struggle), the resulting proposals will be misleading. There is no analysis of proposal quality conditioned on correct vs. incorrect intermediate predictions.

**§4.1 Datasets — Preprocessing difference may interact with results.** MFIDDR images are preprocessed following APTOS 2019 conventions (green channel normalization, circular cropping), while DRTiD uses raw resized images. This difference is standard in the field and is not a flaw, but the paper does not analyze whether the APTOS preprocessing itself aids microlesion detection independently of the proposed modules.

**§4.1 Implementation — Different pretraining for the two datasets.** ImageNet pretraining is used for MFIDDR; EyePACS pretraining for DRTiD (following CrossFiT). The paper states it follows prior work in both cases. However, for end-to-end baselines on DRTiD (MVCNN R/V, DeepDR, Binocular Network, Cv-Transformer), the pretraining is unspecified — if these baselines used ImageNet pretraining when originally published and the paper reproduces them with ImageNet pretraining, the gap of 76.0% vs. 75.6% (CrossFiT) may partially reflect the EyePACS pretraining advantage.

**§4.2 DRTiD results — Claimed improvement is marginal and not tested for significance.** The claimed improvement over CrossFiT on DRTiD is 76.0% vs. 75.6% overall accuracy — a 0.4% gap on a test set of 1,100 eyes (~4 cases). No confidence intervals or variance estimates are reported. The paper presents this as "our end-to-end approach achieves the highest overall accuracy" without acknowledging this difference may be noise.

**§4.3 Hyperparameter study — Retention ratio α = 0.5 is presented as justified by search, but the range is coarse.** The tested values {0.20, 0.50, 0.70, 1.00} are only four points. A performance cliff from 1.00 (all tokens) to 0.70 to 0.50 would be informative for understanding whether the lesion localization actually matters or whether any form of feature selection helps. The figure is described but not reproduced in the extracted text.

**§5 Conclusion — Overclaims practical deployment readiness.** The conclusion states the method "provides a label-efficient and scalable solution for large-scale DR screening." No inference-time latency, memory footprint, or throughput analysis is provided. For a method that introduces MoE routing, multi-stage CAM computation, and cross-view attention at multiple feature resolutions, this claim is unsubstantiated.

---

## Strengths

- **Practical motivation is genuine.** The goal of eliminating inference-time annotation requirements (OD coordinates, vessel masks, lesion maps) is clinically meaningful. Methods like CrossFiT and SMVDR that require auxiliary inputs at test time are genuinely harder to deploy, and the paper correctly identifies this.
- **The "with lesion" variant is a useful addition.** Reporting both a fully annotation-free variant and an annotation-augmented variant on the same architecture makes it possible to measure how much information is captured by self-generated proposals versus ground truth. This design choice is intellectually honest.
- **LGRF's Top-K weighted cross-view attention is a well-specified mechanism.** The formulation of routing scores via the current view's aggregated tokens to gate adjacent-view expert activations (Eq. 9–16) is clearly presented, and the load-balancing loss is appropriate.
- **Two-dataset evaluation provides some generalization evidence.** Evaluating on both a 4-view dataset (MFIDDR) and a 2-view dataset (DRTiD) — with different imaging protocols, sample sizes, and ground truth types — is better practice than evaluating on one dataset alone.

---

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments

1. **Swin-B + naive multi-view concatenation baseline.** Without this anchor, the ablation cannot attribute performance above prior baselines to the proposed modules vs. the backbone. Add this as a "baseline" row in Table 4. If this baseline already exceeds MVCINN and ETMC, the claimed contributions must be revised accordingly.

2. **Proposal localization quality against MFIDDR lesion masks.** Measure Top-K proposal recall and precision against the provided lesion segmentation masks at multiple retention ratios. Without this, the "lesion proposal" claim is unverified. If proposals do not localize lesions better than random top-K spatial selection, the mechanism is doing something other than what is claimed.

3. **All-pairs cross-view fusion ablation.** Compare the cyclic fusion design against full all-pairs cross-view attention (all N×(N-1) pairs) on MFIDDR. If cyclic fusion matches all-pairs despite only seeing one adjacent view, that's a meaningful efficiency finding; if all-pairs is significantly better, the cyclic design is a limitation that should be disclosed.

### Deeper Analysis Needed

1. **Grade-stratified proposal quality analysis.** The model's most significant failure mode is minority grades (Grade 1 and Grade 4 across all methods, visible in Table 2). Analyze whether GALP proposals at these grades correspond to clinically plausible regions. If the auxiliary classifier frequently misclassifies Grade 1 as Grade 0 (common), the proposals generated for Grade 1 cases will be Grade 0-conditioned, which likely misleads fusion.

2. **Sensitivity to auxiliary classifier accuracy.** GALP's proposals are derived from auxiliary classifiers at stages s1–s3. If early classifiers are poorly calibrated (e.g., for low-contrast microaneurysms in Grade 1), the proposals fed into LGRF will be systematically wrong. A quantitative analysis of auxiliary classifier accuracy vs. proposal quality vs. final grade accuracy would clarify whether GALP actually helps for the hardest cases (the ones it claims to address).

### Visualizations & Case Studies

1. **Side-by-side visualization of GEM proposals vs. provided lesion masks.** Show 10–15 representative cases (including true positives, false positives, and grade-misclassified cases) where GEM proposals are overlaid on fundus images alongside ground-truth lesion annotations. This is the single most informative visualization the paper could provide, and its absence is conspicuous given that the MFIDDR dataset has annotation data.

### Obvious Next Steps

1. **Backbone-controlled comparison across all baselines.** Re-implement MVCINN, CVSA, and ETMC with Swin-B backbones to isolate architecture-level gains from method-level gains. This is necessary to make the comparative claims credible and would substantially change (or confirm) the paper's conclusions.

---

## Overall Assessment

The paper proposes a reasonable combination of CAM-based proposal generation (GALP) and MoE-gated cross-view attention (LGRF) for multi-view DR grading, motivated by the practical desire to remove inference-time annotation requirements. The method is clearly described and the experiments span two benchmarks. However, the central comparative claim — that the proposed framework matches or surpasses annotation-using methods without external annotations — is not established by the current evidence, because (a) no baseline uses the same Swin-B backbone as the proposed method, making backbone-controlled comparisons impossible; (b) the claimed lesion-localization property of GALP proposals is never validated against the ground-truth lesion masks that are explicitly available in the MFIDDR benchmark; and (c) the ablation structure does not include a plain Swin-B multi-view fusion baseline, so the incremental contribution of the two proposed modules above a strong backbone cannot be quantified. The cyclic cross-view fusion design is unmotivated and unablated. Margins on DRTiD are within noise (0.4%) and are presented as definitive without statistical testing. The paper should not be accepted in its current form. The core ideas have merit, but the paper's claims are systematically overstated relative to what the evidence can support — primarily due to the failure to control for backbone strength and the unvalidated lesion-proposal framing.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Achieves performance competitive with or superior to externally-informed methods without requiring any external annotations**: On MFIDDR (Table 1), the annotation-free variant "Ours (w/o lesion)" achieves 83.9% Acc and 70.9% Kappa, surpassing externally-informed LFMVDR (82.2% Acc, 66.9% Kappa) and CVSA (82.6% Acc, 67.9% Kappa). On DRTiD (Table 3), the annotation-free model achieves 76.0% Acc, beating even CrossFiT (75.6% Acc), which requires OD and macula coordinates at inference.

2. **Self-derived lesion proposals effectively replace costly external annotations**: The GALP module generates grade-conditioned evidence maps from auxiliary classifiers (Eq. 3) and selects Top-K high-activation regions as lesion proposals (Eq. 5–7), creating a principled internal surrogate for external lesion/vessel masks. The ablation in Table 4 confirms that removing GALP drops accuracy from 83.9% to 82.7%, validating that these self-generated proposals provide meaningful guidance rather than being decorative.

3. **Cross-view expert routing provides selective, context-aware fusion**: The LGRF module gates expert activation based on current-view context (Eq. 9), so only relevant extractors process adjacent-view proposals. Ablation (Table 4) shows removing experts drops Acc from 83.9% to 82.6% and F1 from 83.5% to 81.4%, confirming that expert-based selective routing outperforms naïve cross-attention on proposal tokens.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Sets new SOTA on MFIDDR when lesion annotations are available**: The "Ours (with lesion)" variant achieves 84.6% Acc, 72.3% Kappa, and 84.4% F1 (Table 1), exceeding all prior methods including SMVDR-M (84.0% Acc, 71.4% Kappa) and WGLIN (84.2% Acc, 71.2% Kappa), demonstrating that the architecture is complementary to (not a replacement for) external cues.

2. **Particularly strong gains on clinically critical difficult grades**: On Grade 3 (severe DR) in Table 2, the annotation-free model achieves F1=74.1%, surpassing externally-informed CVSA (73.2%) and LFMVDR (70.9%). This is significant because severe DR is the grade most critical for timely intervention.

3. **Eliminates inference-time dependence on auxiliary models**: Unlike CVSA (needs vessel masks) or SMVDR/LFMVDR (need lesion segmentation maps at inference), this framework is fully self-contained at deployment, avoiding cascading errors from upstream segmentation models — a practical advantage highlighted in Section 1's discussion of "dependency and brittleness."

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Systematic hyperparameter analysis**: Fig. 3 examines retention ratio α and expert count K₂/M, showing that α=50% and K₂=2 out of M=6 experts provide the best accuracy-redundancy trade-off, giving practitioners clear configuration guidance.

2. **Single-view comparison in Appendix (Table 5)**: Demonstrates a large margin over single-view baselines (83.9% vs. best single-view 77.0% Vim), reinforcing the value of the multi-view fusion design.

## Summary

The paper proposes an end-to-end multi-view DR grading framework (GALP + LGRF) that self-generates lesion proposals from internal feature maps, eliminating the need for costly external annotations while matching or surpassing annotation-dependent methods. The single most important piece of evidence is Table 1 on MFIDDR, where the annotation-free model (83.9% Acc, 70.9% Kappa) outperforms multiple externally-informed baselines including LFMVDR (82.2% Acc, 66.9% Kappa) and CVSA (82.6% Acc, 67.9% Kappa), directly confirming that internally derived lesion proposals can serve as effective surrogates for expert-provided annotations.

